279: %% The following is adapted from code in the acmconf.sty conference
280: %% style file.  The constants in it are somewhat magical, and appear
281: %% to work well with the two-column format on US letter paper that
282: %% ICML uses, but will break if you change that layout, or if you use
283: %% a longer block of text for the copyright notice string.  Fiddle with
284: %% them if necessary to get the block to fit/look right.
285: %%
286: %% -- Terran Lane, 2003
287: %%
288: %% The following comments are included verbatim from acmconf.sty:
289: %%
290: %%% This section (written by KBT) handles the 1" box in the lower left
291: %%% corner of the left column of the first page by creating a picture,
292: %%% and inserting the predefined string at the bottom (with a negative
293: %%% displacement to offset the space allocated for a non-existent
294: %%% caption).
295: %%%
296: \def\ftype@copyrightbox{8}
297: \def\@copyrightspace{
298: % Create a float object positioned at the bottom of the column.  Note
299: % that because of the mystical nature of floats, this has to be called
300: % before the first column is populated with text (e.g., from the title
301: % or abstract blocks).  Otherwise, the text will force the float to
302: % the next column.  -- TDRL.
303: \@float{copyrightbox}[b]
304: \begin{center}
305: \setlength{\unitlength}{1pc}
306: \begin{picture}(20,1.5)
307: % Create a line separating the main text from the note block.
308: % 4.818pc==0.8in.
309: \put(0,2.5){\line(1,0){4.818}}
310: % Insert the text string itself.  Note that the string has to be
311: % enclosed in a parbox -- the \put call needs a box object to
312: % position.  Without the parbox, the text gets splattered across the
313: % bottom of the page semi-randomly.  The 19.75pc distance seems to be
314: % the width of the column, though I can't find an appropriate distance
315: % variable to substitute here.  -- TDRL.
316: \put(0,0){\parbox[b]{19.75pc}{\small \Notice@String}}
317: \end{picture}
318: \end{center}
319: \end@float}
